00:24:59,110 --> 00:25:00,230
We're not happier.

192
00:25:00,590 --> 00:25:06,930
And that is global, whether it's in China, whether it's in the UK or Australia or the United States.

193
00:25:08,130 --> 00:25:21,010
Levels of happiness are essentially static and anxiety levels and depression levels, as we talked about in the first class, have gone up significantly.

194
00:25:21,570 --> 00:25:25,490
So income levels make very little difference.

195
00:25:25,930 --> 00:25:32,930
In general, external circumstances make very little difference. I mean, think about your own experience. How did you feel?

196
00:25:33,170 --> 00:25:41,410
Go back, just transport yourself to the experience of getting into Harvard, getting this big package in the mail.

197
00:25:42,290 --> 00:25:49,310
You said you were accepted on April 1 or end of December when you got this package. How did you feel?

198
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:52,880
Probably ecstatic.

199
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:55,600
Probably one of your highlights.

200
00:25:56,040 --> 00:25:59,660
And at that moment, if you're like me, you thought, this is it.

201
00:26:00,200 --> 00:26:10,080
Now I'm really going to be happy for a very long time because I really struggled in high school. A lot of it was difficult. A lot of it was painful. But it was all worth it.

202
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:11,620
I made it.

203
00:26:13,160 --> 00:26:18,220
And you went along with that feeling the next day because in school they started to talk about how you got in.

204
00:26:18,940 --> 00:26:20,940
And you felt fantastic about it, right?

205
00:26:22,820 --> 00:26:29,400
And you felt great for probably the rest of senior year. I mean, still ups and downs, but overall, high levels of happiness.

206
00:26:29,880 --> 00:26:37,360
And it was going to last for the rest of your life. And when you came here freshman week, you knew that it was going to last for the rest of your life.

207
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:41,700
Because you were around wonderful people and it was fun and there were parties.

208
00:26:45,010 --> 00:26:48,970
And you said, everything they said about Harvard was not true.

209
00:26:49,550 --> 00:26:50,830
It's actually a party school.

210
00:26:52,470 --> 00:26:59,990
You were certain of that, right? And not only was that a party school, you were going to have an amazing time for the four years and beyond

211
00:26:59,990 --> 00:27:06,070
because your life was transformed by this letter of acceptance, right?

212
00:27:07,770 --> 00:27:16,430
Things started to change probably on the first day of class, but not entirely because there's shopping period and it's such a wonderful thing. You shop for classes.

213
00:27:17,190 --> 00:27:19,310
It's even better than going to the mall.

214
00:27:20,390 --> 00:27:22,610
So many, 3,000 classes to choose from.

215
00:27:23,510 --> 00:27:30,370
And the second week at Harvard was fantastic. And it was going to continue just that way, a constant high for the rest of your life.

216
00:27:30,730 --> 00:27:33,210
And then suddenly things begin to change.

217
00:27:33,990 --> 00:27:34,590
Slightly.

218
00:27:35,170 --> 00:27:36,390
Ever so slightly.

219
00:27:37,830 --> 00:27:41,810
And they completely change once your first paper is due.

220
00:27:42,850 --> 00:27:44,590
Or the midterms arrive.

221
00:27:45,650 --> 00:27:53,630
and you go back to your base level of well-being. If you experienced a lot of stress in high school and elementary school

222
00:27:53,630 --> 00:28:04,120
and were unhappy, generally the best predictor would say that you would feel the same way a month into your Harvard experience.

223
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:11,720
External circumstances matter very little, very little.

224
00:28:12,800 --> 00:28:18,240
In fact, also, place of residence makes very little difference.

225
00:28:19,220 --> 00:28:24,280
People think, well, if I move, especially on a day like this, if I move to California, I'll be happier, right?

226
00:28:25,200 --> 00:28:28,720
Wrong. Californians are no happier than people in Massachusetts.

227
00:28:30,540 --> 00:28:36,400
Initially, when we go to a warm place, we feel the relief. There is this spike in our levels of well-being.

228
00:28:36,780 --> 00:28:42,580
But very quickly, we go back to our base level, exactly where we were before.

229
00:28:44,420 --> 00:28:49,920
I'll take this even a step further. Even though there is no research about this, I bet you this is correct.

230
00:28:50,260 --> 00:29:02,810
There is no difference in our levels of well-being if our place of residence is by the river or in the quad.

231
00:29:03,490 --> 00:29:07,090
This is really taking the theory far, but it's true.

232
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:14,720
I love this T-shirt. What, if you can't run with the big dogs, stay in the yard? Anyone from Courier here?

233
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:16,760
All right.

234
00:29:19,810 --> 00:29:23,910
You made it all the way on such a cold day. Well done. I appreciate it.

235
00:29:26,260 --> 00:29:27,860
How spoiled we get here.

236
00:29:29,260 --> 00:29:30,720
Makes very little difference.

237
00:29:31,120 --> 00:29:32,340
Very little difference.

238
00:29:33,980 --> 00:29:42,220
Where we are, where we live, income levels, lottery, tenure, getting into our dream college,

239
00:29:42,700 --> 00:29:46,360
getting our dream job for you seniors. You may have just gotten it.

240
00:29:48,610 --> 00:29:54,530
Yeah, you're going to have a spike in your level of well-being. I'm sure you had a spike in your level of well-being when you got this acceptance letter.

241
00:29:54,930 --> 00:29:57,470
But very quickly, we go back to our base level.

242
00:30:00,040 --> 00:30:09,700
One thing that does matter to our base level of well-being, which is an external circumstance, is democracy versus oppression.

243
00:30:09,900 --> 00:30:15,120
People living in democracies are generally significantly happier than people living in dictatorships.

244
00:30:17,320 --> 00:30:23,940
Women, for example, living in places where they're oppressed are in general less happy than women living in free countries.

245
00:30:24,760 --> 00:30:33,000
People living in Darfur are certainly less happy than people living in Denmark or the United States.

246
00:30:34,970 --> 00:30:38,830
But again, these are the extreme circumstances that do make a difference.

247
00:30:39,470 --> 00:30:43,950
Such as I said, a homeless person, of course, income will make a difference to that person.

248
00:30:45,130 --> 00:30:56,470
Moving to a free country, of course, will make a difference to their well-being. But beyond the extremes, additions or subtractions to our external circumstances make very little difference.

249
00:30:57,570 --> 00:31:02,020
Now, this is good news and bad news.

250
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:12,120
The bad news is it seems like whatever we do doesn't matter. So why am I working hard in order to get that BCG job?

251
00:31:13,220 --> 00:31:18,040
Why am I working hard in order to, why did I work so hard in order to get into this place?

252
00:31:18,860 --> 00:31:22,340
if it doesn't matter if I experience these ups and downs?

253
00:31:24,380 --> 00:31:28,000
And the answer is that, yes, it will make a difference to our well-being,

254
00:31:28,660 --> 00:31:33,200
but it also doesn't mean that we cannot increase our levels of well-being.

255
00:31:33,640 --> 00:31:42,340
Many people say that the problem with general levels of happiness, or unhappiness rather, the cause is that people have too high expectations.

256
00:31:43,100 --> 00:31:47,480
And if we lower our expectations, we will lower our stress levels

257
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:59,600
and we will enjoy life more. Okay, so I don't care if I get a B in a course. I'll be much happier if I don't care, if I lower my levels of expectation. I don't care where I get into, what job I have.

258
00:31:59,720 --> 00:32:00,740
I just want to be happy.

259
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:06,840
And you'll probably be happy, slightly happy, if you lower your levels of expectations, but not in the long term.

260
00:32:07,480 --> 00:32:09,200
And we'll talk about it next week.

261
00:32:09,580 --> 00:32:10,680
Not in the long term.

262
00:32:11,900 --> 00:32:16,560
The problem is not low versus high expectations. That doesn't matter.

263
00:32:17,220 --> 00:32:21,300
The problem is right versus wrong expectations.

264
00:32:22,760 --> 00:32:34,480
Not low versus high expectations that won't make a difference to our levels of well-being. What will make a difference to our levels of well-being is if we have right versus wrong expectations.

265
00:32:34,480 --> 00:32:52,700
It is a wrong expectation to expect that getting into a certain place, getting a raise, finding our dream partner is what's responsible for our happiness.

266
00:32:53,420 --> 00:32:58,820
Moving to California or to a four-timer.

267
00:32:59,800 --> 00:33:02,420
That in and of itself will not make us happier.

268
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:11,740
That's wrong expectation. The right expectation is to believe in change from within.

269
00:33:12,100 --> 00:33:14,560
So these things will not make us happy.

270
00:33:15,140 --> 00:33:24,840
In fact, our readiness and potential to experience happiness is mostly dependent on our state of mind, not on our status or the state of our bank account.

271
00:33:25,740 --> 00:33:29,040
It's about changing our perception, state of mind.

272
00:33:29,040 --> 00:33:38,020
It's about changing our interpretation of the world, of what's happening to us, of our achievements, of our failures.

273
00:33:39,460 --> 00:33:43,080
It's about what we choose to perceive, what we choose to focus on.

274
00:33:43,440 --> 00:33:51,900
It's about transformation as opposed to the external information or the external success.

275
00:33:53,880 --> 00:33:59,900
1504, this whole course, is about this transformation, as I mentioned during the first lesson.

276
00:34:09,410 --> 00:34:19,990
I want to move on to the fourth premise, one of the most important premises in this course. And in many ways, we can understand human intellectual history just based on this idea.

277
00:34:20,690 --> 00:34:27,090
Should we accept human nature or can we perfect it and can it change?

278
00:34:27,880 --> 00:34:35,110
This work relies on, well, these ideas rely on the work of Thomas Sowell.

279
00:34:35,290 --> 00:34:43,270
Thomas Sowell, Harvard class of 58, now at Stanford, the Hoover Institute, one of my intellectual heroes.

280
00:34:44,030 --> 00:34:55,850
And what he does, and what his work does, is basically explain why people choose to back one political party versus the other, why they choose to live one kind of life versus the other.

281
00:34:55,850 --> 00:35:04,730
this single book helped me understand myself better, as well as other people better, whether it's politically, whether it's psychologically, whether it's philosophically. Those of you

282
00:35:04,730 --> 00:35:08,890
interested in politics and there is one book you want to read, after you read Marva Collins,

283
00:35:09,030 --> 00:35:17,250
of course, it's this. So what does Thomas Sowell say? Essentially what he does is divide people

284
00:35:18,030 --> 00:35:25,550
into two camps. Those who hold the constrained vision, the limited vision, and those who

285
00:35:25,550 --> 00:35:33,330
hold the unconstrained, the unlimited vision of human nature. People who hold the constrained vision

286
00:35:34,110 --> 00:35:40,710
of human nature believe that human nature cannot be changed. It's immutable. We have certain

287
00:35:40,710 --> 00:35:46,450
instincts. We have certain inclinations. They're fixed. This is it. What you see is what you get.

288
00:35:46,570 --> 00:35:55,690
What you are born with, what we're born with as a species is all we have. The flaws that are there are inevitable. They cannot be changed.

289
00:35:56,970 --> 00:35:59,790
We need to accept them for what they are.

290
00:36:00,470 --> 00:36:07,750
That's the only thing we can do with these flaws. And people who have the constrained vision, who thinks that our instincts, our inclinations,

291
00:36:08,170 --> 00:36:11,450
our basic nature is immutable,

292
00:36:11,950 --> 00:36:22,810
accept it. And then what they do, rather than resign, they channel it. How do they channel it? By building certain political institutions that would channel

293
00:36:22,810 --> 00:36:26,490
the flawed, imperfect human nature toward the good.

294
00:36:28,150 --> 00:36:38,870
Philosophers and psychologists who fall under the constrained vision create then philosophies and psychologies and institutions and systems

295
00:36:38,870 --> 00:36:43,350
to channel our flawed and imperfect nature.

296
00:36:43,770 --> 00:36:51,690
People who fall under the constrained vision in the history of ideas are people such as Alexander Hamilton.

297
00:36:52,810 --> 00:36:56,410
Adam Smith, those of you who are taken or have taken Act 10.

298
00:36:59,250 --> 00:37:01,590
Ayn Rand, Friedrich Hayek.

299
00:37:05,110 --> 00:37:11,890
These are people who say our nature is constrained, it's limited. Edmund Burke, another one.

300
00:37:13,700 --> 00:37:25,580
What captures this idea best? The words of Francis Bacon, considered the father of the scientific movement, was a philosopher in the 1600s.

301
00:37:25,740 --> 00:37:33,360
Nature to be commanded must be obeyed. Whether it's physical nature or human nature.

302
00:37:33,800 --> 00:37:36,620
We need to obey it. It is what it is.

303
00:37:37,140 --> 00:37:39,100
Now we have the unconstrained vision.

304
00:37:40,340 --> 00:37:44,720
Bless you, a little bit more optimistic, a little bit more utopian.

305
00:37:45,900 --> 00:37:50,860
Human nature can be improved. It can be changed. It's up to us to do so.

306
00:37:51,480 --> 00:37:57,460
It is perfectible. We don't need to accept these flaws as inevitable. We can perfect it.

307
00:37:58,380 --> 00:38:02,320
there are solutions to these flaws, to these imperfections.

308
00:38:02,780 --> 00:38:08,460
And our role, the goal of institutions, whether it's political institutions,

309
00:38:08,680 --> 00:38:16,520
educational institutions, of systems, organizations, individuals, philosophers, and psychologists,

310
00:38:16,660 --> 00:38:20,860
the role is to change our nature, to perfect it, to better it.

311
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:26,100
Philosophers who held this position, people like Thomas Jefferson.

312
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:41,030
People like Jean-Jacques Rousseau, George Bernard Shaw, Ronald Dworkin, and other leading thinkers.

313
00:38:41,370 --> 00:38:52,310
And what Thomas Sowell did was map the entire intellectual history of the world, showing how people who held this view versus that view led to radically different political prescriptions.

314
00:38:53,210 --> 00:38:58,570
The person who best captures the unconstrained view, Benjamin Constant, a French philosopher and politician.

315
00:38:59,110 --> 00:39:03,090
It is for self-perfectioning that destiny calls us.

316
00:39:03,830 --> 00:39:14,820
Generally, people on the constrained view are people politically, not always, but usually are people who would support capitalism.

317
00:39:15,780 --> 00:39:18,740
People like Adam Smith, who talks about the invisible hand.

318
00:39:19,240 --> 00:39:25,280
Let's channel imperfect, perhaps flawed human nature toward the good.

319
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:32,900
People who are more on the unconstrained vision would tend toward utopianism or sometimes communism.

320
00:39:32,900 --> 00:39:42,760
Not always, but sometimes. Why? Because let's change human nature. Self-interest is not good. It hurts in the long term, and therefore we need to change it.

321
00:39:43,720 --> 00:39:54,300
Rather than the constrained vision, people who said, we may not like it, but it is what it is. We cannot change it. Let's make the best of it and channel it toward the good.

322
00:39:54,300 --> 00:40:02,900
to radical views, to radical prescriptions based on people's notion perspective.

323
00:40:04,220 --> 00:40:08,220
So why am I telling you this? I mean, this is not a political science class

324
00:40:09,420 --> 00:40:13,620
because it has every relevance to psychology as well.

325
00:40:14,260 --> 00:40:16,700
It has every relevance to psychology.

326
00:40:17,300 --> 00:40:25,840
Do we perceive reality, human nature, as constrained or unconstrained will ultimately affect our psychology, And this is very meaningful and important.

327
00:40:26,400 --> 00:40:27,440
Let me explain.

328
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:38,140
Now, first of all, some people who have the constrained view when it comes to politics have the unconstrained view when it comes to psychology. It's not always consistent, though it very often is.

329
00:40:40,580 --> 00:40:52,100
So the vision of psychology that this course promotes is the constrained view. In other words, my belief, and I'm going to back that up with research today

330
00:40:52,100 --> 00:41:01,200
and for the next semester, is that human nature is fixed. We have inclinations, we have certain instincts that are there

331
00:41:01,200 --> 00:41:10,420
and they have been formed either God-given or evolutionary-given for millions of years, they have been formed. They're not going to change any time soon, not in our lifetime.

332
00:41:11,360 --> 00:41:12,340
It's fixed.

333
00:41:12,720 --> 00:41:21,180
We're stuck with them for good and ill. And all we can do after we accept this nature is to first understand it

334
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:32,140
through research, and then, after we understand it, make the best use of it. Understand it through research as well as through the search, through introspection,

335
00:41:36,440 --> 00:41:39,940
and then make best use of it. How can I channel my nature?

336
00:41:40,600 --> 00:41:47,440
And what I want to do now is illustrate a case study of what I mean by nature constraint

337
00:41:47,440 --> 00:41:52,620
and why it is so important for happiness, for well-being, for long-term success

338
00:41:53,360 --> 00:41:58,480
to have the constrained view of human nature when it comes to our psychology, that is.

339
00:42:00,080 --> 00:42:03,880
The topic I want to talk about is the permission to be human.

340
00:42:06,890 --> 00:42:16,430
I'm going to talk about this topic for three reasons. A, because it illustrates what Thomas Sowell means politically in the psychological realm.

341
00:42:18,430 --> 00:42:23,590
Second, because I think it is one of the most important pillars of well-being and happiness.

342
00:42:24,290 --> 00:42:35,870
And the third reason why I'm going to talk about it is because it gets to a misunderstanding that many people have regarding what positive psychology is and is not.

343
00:42:39,310 --> 00:42:48,830
When I first started teaching this course and had the six students, I remember one day sitting in Leverett House having lunch by myself.

344
00:42:48,830 --> 00:42:57,010
And one of the students comes in and says, can I join you? Sure, we have lunch. And then he says to me, Tal, I hear you're teaching a class on happiness.

345
00:42:57,710 --> 00:42:59,370
And I said to him, yes, that's right.

346
00:43:00,730 --> 00:43:04,250
And then he said, you know, my roommates are taking your class.

347
00:43:04,770 --> 00:43:08,110
And I said, that's wonderful. You know, there were two of the six.

348
00:43:09,830 --> 00:43:12,590
And then he said, but Tal, you've got to watch out now.

349
00:43:13,690 --> 00:43:15,110
And I said to him, why?

350
00:43:15,490 --> 00:43:18,450
And he said, Tal, you've got to be careful. And I said, why?

351
00:43:19,270 --> 00:43:23,210
And he said, because if I see you unhappy, I'll tell them.

352
00:43:25,960 --> 00:43:34,040
Now, the next day in class, I used that and I said to the students, you know, the last thing in the world that I want you to think is that I experience a constant high.

353
00:43:37,150 --> 00:43:43,910
Or that you, by the end of the semester or the year, it was a year course, you, by the end of the year, will experience a constant high.

354
00:43:44,910 --> 00:43:56,230
There are two kinds of people who experience this constant high who don't experience painful emotions, such as anger or envy or disappointment or sadness

355
00:43:56,230 --> 00:43:59,670
or unhappiness or depression or anxiety at times.

356
00:44:00,190 --> 00:44:07,170
Two kinds of people who don't experience these painful emotions. The one kind are the psychopaths.

357
00:44:07,770 --> 00:44:16,990
By definition, they don't experience these painful emotions. The second kind of people who don't experience these painful emotions are dead people.

358
00:44:17,370 --> 00:44:17,910
Exactly.

359
00:44:18,510 --> 00:44:23,970
So, you know, if you experience these emotions, it's a good sign.

360
00:44:24,350 --> 00:44:27,250
You're not a psychopath and you're alive.

361
00:44:29,540 --> 00:44:39,240
However, in our culture today, we don't give ourselves the permission to be human, the freedom to experience these painful emotions as well.

362
00:44:39,240 --> 00:44:48,680
And we pay a very high price for this inability, for this refusal to accept the fact that it's part of human nature,

363
00:44:48,680 --> 00:44:52,560
that is constrained, that is there no matter what.

364
00:44:55,540 --> 00:45:00,600
As kids, as babies, going back to the baby's theme, we give ourselves the permission to be human.

365
00:45:01,640 --> 00:45:07,560
We know that it's natural. We don't even think about it, in fact, to experience high and then low and then high and then low.

366
00:45:08,300 --> 00:45:13,620
And later on, when we stop giving ourselves the permission to be human, when the facade becomes so important,

367
00:45:13,980 --> 00:45:18,240
when we begin to realize that other people are watching us and evaluating us constantly,

368
00:45:19,740 --> 00:45:23,060
and we think they do so much more than they actually do,

369
00:45:25,060 --> 00:45:31,760
that's when we stop giving ourselves the permission to be human and we pay a price in terms of our energy levels,

370
00:45:33,040 --> 00:45:36,080
of our well-being, of our happiness, of our creativity,

371
00:45:36,760 --> 00:45:38,600
and ultimately also our success.

372
00:45:39,620 --> 00:45:44,700
Let me give you an example of someone who does give himself the permission to be human.

373
00:46:37,580 --> 00:46:40,080
Now, I'm not saying we should look like this.

374
00:46:41,120 --> 00:46:51,480
But what I am saying is that we all need a space, a place in our lives where we give ourselves the permission to be human. Whether it's with close friends, people we care about.

375
00:46:51,980 --> 00:46:55,540
Whether it's first and foremost with ourselves, when we write a journal.

376
00:46:56,780 --> 00:47:00,960
where we do give ourselves the permission to be, to cry, to be joyous.

377
00:47:01,980 --> 00:47:08,840
Because if we don't, we pay a price. We need a space of unconditional acceptance.

378
00:47:10,360 --> 00:47:19,400
The best advice that I got or that we got, my wife and I, when David, our first son, was born, was from our pediatrician, Dr. Todd Shapiro.

379
00:47:20,080 --> 00:47:32,080
And David was born at 1 a.m. in the morning. And then at around 8 a.m. in the morning, he came in to see, to check up on my wife, to check up on the baby. For some reason, he didn't check up on me.

380
00:47:34,020 --> 00:47:40,480
And everything was fine. And as he was leaving the room, he turned around and said, just one more thing.

381
00:47:41,520 --> 00:47:49,540
Over the next few months, you're going to be experiencing every single kind of emotion to the extreme.

382
00:47:51,560 --> 00:47:54,560
And that's fine. It's natural. We all go through it.

383
00:47:55,740 --> 00:48:02,300
And then he walked out. It was the best advice that my wife and I got regarding child rearing. Why? Let me give you an example.

384
00:48:02,600 --> 00:48:11,100
So, after about a month, I started to, once in a while, experience some envy toward David.

385
00:48:13,120 --> 00:48:24,660
